<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 53 0 R
/T (Section 3 The Empty Air of Machlokes: This brings us to the profound conceptual difference between chametz and matzah as it relates to our family dynamics. The Kli Yakar \(Shemos 12:15\) explains that chametz represents the yetzer hara \(evil inclination\), which by its very nature causes division, separation, and machlokes \(dispute\). Why does dough rise? It rises because trapped air creates pockets of emptiness that literally push the ingredients apart from the inside out. Matzah, conversely, is flat, dense, and unified. It represents the humility required to stay close together. In our community, it is sometimes much easier to write a generous check to buy the shul a new heavy-duty safe or a set of tables than it is to secure the spiritual safety of our own dining rooms. The Seder night demands that we return to the state of matzah. It asks us to deflate our egos, to sit together in tight quarters without letting the empty air of pride push us apart. Only when a home is unified in the humility of matzah can it become a place where the next generation feels comfortable enough to ask the Mah Nishtanah and actually care about the answer.)
/E (Section 3 The Empty Air of Machlokes: This brings us to the profound conceptual difference between chametz and matzah as it relates to our family dynamics. The Kli Yakar \(Shemos 12:15\) explains that chametz represents the yetzer hara \(evil inclination\), which by its very nature causes division, separation, and machlokes \(dispute\). Why does dough rise? It rises because trapped air creates pockets of emptiness that literally push the ingredients apart from the inside out. Matzah, conversely, is flat, dense, and unified. It represents the humility required to stay close together. In our community, it is sometimes much easier to write a generous check to buy the shul a new heavy-duty safe or a set of tables than it is to secure the spiritual safety of our own dining rooms. The Seder night demands that we return to the state of matzah. It asks us to deflate our egos, to sit together in tight quarters without letting the empty air of pride push us apart. Only when a home is unified in the humility of matzah can it become a place where the next generation feels comfortable enough to ask the Mah Nishtanah and actually care about the answer.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
